<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhutia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhutia are a people of Tibetan origin in Sikkim and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, whose ancestors migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in medieval times. They speak the Sikkimese language and follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. From 1642 Bhutia nobles and lamas formed the ruling class of the Namgyal kingdom of Sikkim, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lepcha">Lepcha</a> and later the Nepali communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhutia.jpg" alt="Bhutia" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhutia_in_the_1860s_detail,_from-_Portret_van_een_onbekende_Bhutia_man_uit_Tibet_Bhotia._Buddhist._Tibet_(titel_op_object),_RP-F-2001-7-1122A-55_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bhutias call themselves Drejongpas, People of the Rice Valley, and their language, Drejongke, is a Tibetic language descended from Old Tibetan, closer to Dzongkha, the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>, than to Standard Tibetan. Small numbers migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> to Sikkim from the 8th century, and in the 13th century many clans came with Gyed Bum Sa. Conflicts between the Red Hat and Yellow Hat sects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> drove a series of lamas southwards.</p><p>After the final victory of the Yellow Hats in the mid-1600s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> allies persecuted followers of the Red Hat sect, and many fled through the passes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> into Sikkim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>, where Red Hat majorities remain. In North Sikkim, where Bhutias are the majority, they are known as Lachenpas or Lachungpas, after La chen, the big pass, and La chung, the small pass.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bhutia aristocrats bore the title Kazi, after landlord titles in neighbouring regions, especially present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. This feudal order was part of the Chogyal monarchy, and the Bhutia Namgyal dynasty ruled the Kingdom of Sikkim until the plebiscite of the mid-1970s. Kazi Lhendup Dorjee, a Bhutia, became the first chief minister of Sikkim in 1975 and held the post until 1979. The Lachenpas and Lachungpas keep their own legal system, the Dzumsa, the meeting place of the people, headed by a village headman called the Pipon. The Bhutias are recognised as a Scheduled Tribe in Sikkim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditionally the Bhutias were divided into two groups of clans, the Tondu rus-shi with four rus and the Beb tsen gye with eight, and marriage outside the community is looked down upon. They follow Vajrayana Buddhism, mainly the Nyingma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu schools</a>. Losar marks the Tibetan New Year, and Losoong, at the end of the tenth lunar month, is their most important festival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> dancing. The Rumtek Monastery in Sikkim is among their best-known monasteries.</p><p>The traditional garment is the kho, a loose cloak fastened at the neck and waist; a married woman wears the pangden, a multicoloured woollen cloth, in front. A house, the khim, is usually rectangular, with a vase-shaped stone sangbum outside for burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> to the deities. Chhaang, made of fermented barley or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, is served in a bamboo container called the Tongba.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhutia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1642 three Tibetan lamas consecrated Phuntsog Namgyal as the first Chogyal at Yuksom, founding a Buddhist monarchy over the <b>Bhutia</b> and Lepcha peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sikkim">Kingdom of Sikkim</a></li></ul></section>
